1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,380
Picture the biggest IOU on Earth.

2
00:00:03,380 --> 00:00:05,018
It's signed by the United States

3
00:00:05,018 --> 00:00:06,080
government.

4
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:08,507
It's so long that nobody has ever

5
00:00:08,507 --> 00:00:10,280
read the whole thing.

6
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:12,037
And every single day,

7
00:00:12,037 --> 00:00:13,127
on average,

8
00:00:13,127 --> 00:00:15,352
about six point eight billion dollars more

9
00:00:15,352 --> 00:00:17,509
gets added at the bottom.

10
00:00:17,750 --> 00:00:18,854
On November thirteenth,

11
00:00:18,854 --> 00:00:20,277
the Treasury's own daily count,

12
00:00:20,277 --> 00:00:21,541
called Debt to the Penny,

13
00:00:21,541 --> 00:00:23,560
stood at forty point six trillion dollars.

14
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:26,860
So who actually holds this IOU?

15
00:00:27,110 --> 00:00:29,750
Most people would guess China.

16
00:00:29,970 --> 00:00:32,080
Most people would be wrong.

17
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:32,888
China holds about a penny and a

18
00:00:32,888 --> 00:00:34,040
half of every dollar.

19
00:00:34,140 --> 00:00:37,974
So who holds the rest?

20
00:00:38,217 --> 00:00:40,207
Let's open the vault.

21
00:00:40,207 --> 00:00:41,438
We'll meet the buyers,

22
00:00:41,438 --> 00:00:42,670
the dinner guests,

23
00:00:42,670 --> 00:00:44,906
the warehouse that bought trillions,

24
00:00:44,906 --> 00:00:47,671
and the filing cabinet full of Social

25
00:00:47,671 --> 00:00:49,527
Security's IOUs.

26
00:00:49,557 --> 00:00:51,892
Then we'll check the meter.

27
00:01:07,150 --> 00:01:07,813
First,

28
00:01:07,813 --> 00:01:09,800
what is this debt?

29
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:11,970
It's every Treasury security the

30
00:01:11,970 --> 00:01:13,779
government has issued and not yet paid

31
00:01:13,779 --> 00:01:14,560
back.

32
00:01:14,610 --> 00:01:16,660
It comes in two pieces.

33
00:01:16,790 --> 00:01:19,184
Thirty-two point eight trillion dollars is

34
00:01:19,184 --> 00:01:21,190
debt held by the public.

35
00:01:21,190 --> 00:01:21,661
Bills,

36
00:01:21,661 --> 00:01:24,490
notes and bonds that investors bought.

37
00:01:24,670 --> 00:01:26,662
The other seven point eight trillion

38
00:01:26,662 --> 00:01:29,096
dollars is debt the government owes to

39
00:01:29,096 --> 00:01:29,738
itself,

40
00:01:29,738 --> 00:01:31,444
to its own trust funds.

41
00:01:31,683 --> 00:01:33,063
Let me draw it.

42
00:01:33,063 --> 00:01:35,153
Here's the whole IOU.

43
00:01:35,153 --> 00:01:36,553
Now split it.

44
00:01:36,753 --> 00:01:38,145
The big box is held by the

45
00:01:38,145 --> 00:01:39,143
public.

46
00:01:39,153 --> 00:01:41,033
That means anyone outside the federal

47
00:01:41,033 --> 00:01:41,766
government,

48
00:01:41,766 --> 00:01:44,433
and that includes the Federal Reserve.

49
00:01:44,553 --> 00:01:47,603
The smaller box is intragovernmental.

50
00:01:47,883 --> 00:01:50,004
Social Security's trust funds hold about a

51
00:01:50,004 --> 00:01:50,693
third of it,

52
00:01:50,693 --> 00:01:52,353
by our calculation.

53
00:01:52,623 --> 00:01:54,293
Hold on to that box.

54
00:01:54,293 --> 00:01:55,890
We'll open it later.

55
00:01:56,150 --> 00:01:58,020
And it keeps growing.

56
00:01:58,020 --> 00:01:59,348
One year earlier,

57
00:01:59,348 --> 00:02:01,516
the total was thirty-eight point two

58
00:02:01,516 --> 00:02:03,200
trillion dollars.

59
00:02:03,290 --> 00:02:05,522
That's about two and a half trillion

60
00:02:05,522 --> 00:02:07,482
dollars added in twelve months,

61
00:02:07,482 --> 00:02:09,962
or roughly six point eight billion dollars

62
00:02:09,962 --> 00:02:10,682
a day,

63
00:02:10,682 --> 00:02:12,770
by our calculation.

64
00:02:12,890 --> 00:02:15,351
The counter passed thirty-eight trillion

65
00:02:15,351 --> 00:02:18,058
in October twenty twenty-five,

66
00:02:18,058 --> 00:02:20,218
thirty-nine trillion in March,

67
00:02:20,218 --> 00:02:23,552
and forty trillion on August eighteenth.

68
00:02:23,817 --> 00:02:26,657
So where does new debt come from?

69
00:02:26,657 --> 00:02:27,817
An auction.

70
00:02:27,817 --> 00:02:30,018
In fiscal year twenty twenty-six,

71
00:02:30,018 --> 00:02:32,020
the Treasury held four hundred and

72
00:02:32,020 --> 00:02:33,332
thirty-six auctions,

73
00:02:33,332 --> 00:02:35,333
and sold nearly thirty-three trillion

74
00:02:35,333 --> 00:02:37,090
dollars of securities,

75
00:02:37,090 --> 00:02:38,357
by our count.

76
00:02:38,467 --> 00:02:40,408
Most of that was short-term bills,

77
00:02:40,408 --> 00:02:42,104
paid off and sold again,

78
00:02:42,104 --> 00:02:43,933
over and over.

79
00:02:44,183 --> 00:02:45,863
Take one sale.

80
00:02:45,943 --> 00:02:46,995
On August twelfth,

81
00:02:46,995 --> 00:02:49,573
the Treasury auctioned forty-two billion

82
00:02:49,573 --> 00:02:51,270
dollars of ten-year notes,

83
00:02:51,270 --> 00:02:52,773
and bids came in for more than

84
00:02:52,773 --> 00:02:53,803
twice that.

85
00:02:54,033 --> 00:02:55,627
Of what the bidders won,

86
00:02:55,627 --> 00:02:57,937
about three quarters went to indirect

87
00:02:57,937 --> 00:02:58,607
bidders,

88
00:02:58,607 --> 00:03:00,593
who bid through a dealer.

89
00:03:00,603 --> 00:03:03,383
That group includes foreign central banks.

90
00:03:03,433 --> 00:03:06,373
Direct bidders took about fifteen percent.

91
00:03:06,373 --> 00:03:09,091
Big dealer banks took the rest.

92
00:03:09,350 --> 00:03:11,241
And a lot of that paper is

93
00:03:11,241 --> 00:03:12,550
short-dated.

94
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:14,290
At the end of October,

95
00:03:14,290 --> 00:03:15,276
Treasury bills,

96
00:03:15,276 --> 00:03:16,867
which mature within a year,

97
00:03:16,867 --> 00:03:18,678
made up almost a quarter of the

98
00:03:18,678 --> 00:03:20,872
marketable debt held by the public,

99
00:03:20,872 --> 00:03:22,680
by our calculation.

100
00:03:22,960 --> 00:03:26,010
Short paper has to be refinanced quickly.

101
00:03:26,500 --> 00:03:28,225
Remember that when we get to the

102
00:03:28,225 --> 00:03:29,592
interest bill.

103
00:03:29,850 --> 00:03:32,590
The buyers line up every week.

104
00:03:32,710 --> 00:03:35,061
But who ends up holding the paper?

105
00:03:39,317 --> 00:03:42,219
Picture every foreign holder at one long

106
00:03:42,219 --> 00:03:43,787
dinner table.

107
00:03:43,827 --> 00:03:45,883
In August twenty twenty-six,

108
00:03:45,883 --> 00:03:48,886
according to the Treasury's international

109
00:03:48,886 --> 00:03:50,248
capital data,

110
00:03:50,248 --> 00:03:52,327
foreigners held nine point three trillion

111
00:03:52,327 --> 00:03:55,237
dollars of Treasury securities.

112
00:03:55,527 --> 00:03:57,802
The biggest plate at the table isn't

113
00:03:57,802 --> 00:03:58,907
China's.

114
00:03:58,917 --> 00:04:00,061
It's Japan's,

115
00:04:00,061 --> 00:04:03,827
at one point zero seven trillion dollars.

116
00:04:03,827 --> 00:04:04,567
Second,

117
00:04:04,567 --> 00:04:06,329
the United Kingdom,

118
00:04:06,329 --> 00:04:09,481
at one point zero one trillion dollars.

119
00:04:09,717 --> 00:04:11,217
China comes third,

120
00:04:11,217 --> 00:04:13,759
with five hundred and ninety-one billion

121
00:04:13,759 --> 00:04:14,867
dollars.

122
00:04:14,947 --> 00:04:17,347
And its plate has been shrinking.

123
00:04:17,467 --> 00:04:19,210
Back in twenty thirteen,

124
00:04:19,210 --> 00:04:21,232
China held one point three trillion

125
00:04:21,232 --> 00:04:22,337
dollars.

126
00:04:22,407 --> 00:04:23,236
Today,

127
00:04:23,236 --> 00:04:25,837
it holds less than half of that.

128
00:04:26,083 --> 00:04:29,533
Now compare that with the whole IOU.

129
00:04:29,623 --> 00:04:31,699
China's holdings are about one and a

130
00:04:31,699 --> 00:04:33,547
half percent of the total debt,

131
00:04:33,547 --> 00:04:35,393
by our calculation.

132
00:04:35,623 --> 00:04:36,979
About a penny and a half of

133
00:04:36,979 --> 00:04:38,333
every dollar.

134
00:04:38,503 --> 00:04:40,429
All foreign holders together,

135
00:04:40,429 --> 00:04:42,543
about twenty-three percent.

136
00:04:42,823 --> 00:04:45,622
The idea that China owns America simply

137
00:04:45,622 --> 00:04:47,513
isn't what the data show.

138
00:04:47,750 --> 00:04:49,390
Let me chart it.

139
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:51,513
China in twenty thirteen,

140
00:04:51,513 --> 00:04:52,880
a tall bar.

141
00:04:52,880 --> 00:04:54,182
China today,

142
00:04:54,182 --> 00:04:56,070
less than half as tall.

143
00:04:56,070 --> 00:04:57,239
Japan today,

144
00:04:57,239 --> 00:04:59,510
the largest foreign holder.

145
00:04:59,670 --> 00:05:00,170
Now,

146
00:05:00,170 --> 00:05:01,197
for scale,

147
00:05:01,197 --> 00:05:02,670
the whole debt.

148
00:05:02,670 --> 00:05:04,928
All three bars fit in one corner

149
00:05:04,928 --> 00:05:06,092
of it.

150
00:05:06,350 --> 00:05:07,870
One caveat.

151
00:05:07,870 --> 00:05:09,810
The Treasury itself warns that these

152
00:05:09,810 --> 00:05:12,180
numbers follow where securities are held

153
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:13,236
in custody,

154
00:05:13,236 --> 00:05:15,560
not always who really owns them.

155
00:05:15,910 --> 00:05:17,949
Bonds kept in places like Belgium,

156
00:05:17,949 --> 00:05:20,240
Luxembourg or the Cayman Islands may

157
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:23,360
belong to investors from somewhere else.

158
00:05:23,660 --> 00:05:25,555
So the guest list is a good

159
00:05:25,555 --> 00:05:26,069
map,

160
00:05:26,069 --> 00:05:28,023
not a perfect one.

161
00:05:28,283 --> 00:05:30,581
About three point eight trillion dollars

162
00:05:30,581 --> 00:05:33,389
of the foreign total sits with official

163
00:05:33,389 --> 00:05:34,129
holders,

164
00:05:34,129 --> 00:05:35,635
mostly central banks,

165
00:05:35,635 --> 00:05:38,533
which keep Treasuries as reserves.

166
00:05:38,653 --> 00:05:40,679
When a country needs safe dollars in

167
00:05:40,679 --> 00:05:41,556
a hurry,

168
00:05:41,556 --> 00:05:43,957
this is the pantry it opens.

169
00:05:48,217 --> 00:05:50,193
So if roughly three quarters of the

170
00:05:50,193 --> 00:05:51,268
debt isn't foreign,

171
00:05:51,268 --> 00:05:52,367
who holds it?

172
00:05:52,367 --> 00:05:53,145
Mostly,

173
00:05:53,145 --> 00:05:54,567
Americans.

174
00:05:54,567 --> 00:05:56,987
Here's the Federal Reserve's own

175
00:05:56,987 --> 00:05:57,689
breakdown,

176
00:05:57,689 --> 00:06:00,326
at the end of June twenty twenty-six,

177
00:06:00,326 --> 00:06:02,988
for Treasuries held outside the federal

178
00:06:02,988 --> 00:06:04,314
government.

179
00:06:04,550 --> 00:06:07,257
Money market funds held three point three

180
00:06:07,257 --> 00:06:09,060
trillion dollars.

181
00:06:09,110 --> 00:06:11,960
That's where many people park their cash.

182
00:06:12,060 --> 00:06:14,059
Households held two point eight trillion

183
00:06:14,059 --> 00:06:15,770
dollars directly.

184
00:06:15,950 --> 00:06:18,628
Mutual funds and exchange-traded funds,

185
00:06:18,628 --> 00:06:21,439
about two point six trillion dollars.

186
00:06:21,683 --> 00:06:24,533
Banks and credit unions held about two

187
00:06:24,533 --> 00:06:26,273
trillion dollars.

188
00:06:26,273 --> 00:06:28,113
State and local governments,

189
00:06:28,113 --> 00:06:30,283
one point six trillion dollars.

190
00:06:30,283 --> 00:06:31,288
Pension funds,

191
00:06:31,288 --> 00:06:33,543
one point two trillion dollars.

192
00:06:33,543 --> 00:06:35,089
And insurance companies,

193
00:06:35,089 --> 00:06:37,449
about six hundred billion dollars.

194
00:06:37,683 --> 00:06:40,123
So if you have a retirement plan,

195
00:06:40,123 --> 00:06:40,997
a pension,

196
00:06:40,997 --> 00:06:43,012
a money market fund or a bank

197
00:06:43,012 --> 00:06:43,661
account,

198
00:06:43,661 --> 00:06:45,676
there's a decent chance a slice of

199
00:06:45,676 --> 00:06:47,843
this IOU is working for you.

200
00:06:47,993 --> 00:06:49,926
The interest the government pays doesn't

201
00:06:49,926 --> 00:06:50,703
vanish.

202
00:06:50,703 --> 00:06:52,412
A lot of it lands in American

203
00:06:52,412 --> 00:06:53,354
accounts.

204
00:06:53,617 --> 00:06:55,467
Let me draw the loop.

205
00:06:55,467 --> 00:06:57,777
You put savings into a fund.

206
00:06:57,777 --> 00:06:59,817
The fund lends to the Treasury by

207
00:06:59,817 --> 00:07:01,087
buying a bond.

208
00:07:01,087 --> 00:07:03,214
The Treasury pays interest back to the

209
00:07:03,214 --> 00:07:03,947
fund.

210
00:07:03,947 --> 00:07:05,797
And the fund pays you.

211
00:07:05,947 --> 00:07:08,465
One person's debt is another person's

212
00:07:08,465 --> 00:07:09,568
savings.

213
00:07:09,817 --> 00:07:12,371
Then there's the biggest single holder in

214
00:07:12,371 --> 00:07:13,667
the country.

215
00:07:13,717 --> 00:07:15,537
The Federal Reserve.

216
00:07:15,717 --> 00:07:17,669
On November eleventh,

217
00:07:17,669 --> 00:07:19,645
the Fed held four point six trillion

218
00:07:19,645 --> 00:07:22,338
dollars in Treasury securities,

219
00:07:22,338 --> 00:07:25,056
stacked in the warehouse of its balance

220
00:07:25,056 --> 00:07:25,903
sheet.

221
00:07:26,150 --> 00:07:28,068
That's down from a peak of five

222
00:07:28,068 --> 00:07:30,260
point eight trillion dollars in June

223
00:07:30,260 --> 00:07:32,230
twenty twenty-two.

224
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:34,029
After the pandemic,

225
00:07:34,029 --> 00:07:36,466
the Fed let bonds mature without replacing

226
00:07:36,466 --> 00:07:37,109
them all,

227
00:07:37,109 --> 00:07:39,260
and the shelves thinned out.

228
00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:40,956
But over the past year,

229
00:07:40,956 --> 00:07:42,985
its holdings of short-term bills grew by

230
00:07:42,985 --> 00:07:45,465
about three hundred and eighty-two billion

231
00:07:45,465 --> 00:07:46,516
dollars.

232
00:07:46,750 --> 00:07:47,814
Add it up,

233
00:07:47,814 --> 00:07:48,960
roughly.

234
00:07:48,980 --> 00:07:50,104
Foreign holders,

235
00:07:50,104 --> 00:07:51,850
about a quarter.

236
00:07:51,900 --> 00:07:52,678
The Fed,

237
00:07:52,678 --> 00:07:54,350
about a ninth.

238
00:07:54,460 --> 00:07:55,711
Other investors,

239
00:07:55,711 --> 00:07:57,174
mostly American,

240
00:07:57,174 --> 00:07:58,410
close to half.

241
00:07:58,460 --> 00:08:00,310
And the last big piece?

242
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:03,019
The one the government owes to itself.

243
00:08:07,283 --> 00:08:09,743
Meet the filing cabinet.

244
00:08:09,893 --> 00:08:10,442
For years,

245
00:08:10,442 --> 00:08:12,716
Social Security collected more than it

246
00:08:12,716 --> 00:08:14,453
paid out in benefits.

247
00:08:14,493 --> 00:08:16,888
The surplus was lent to the Treasury,

248
00:08:16,888 --> 00:08:18,866
in exchange for special Treasury

249
00:08:18,866 --> 00:08:21,064
securities held only inside the

250
00:08:21,064 --> 00:08:22,323
government.

251
00:08:22,483 --> 00:08:23,135
IOUs,

252
00:08:23,135 --> 00:08:25,792
filed in a drawer.

253
00:08:26,050 --> 00:08:28,480
At the end of twenty twenty-five,

254
00:08:28,480 --> 00:08:30,690
those trust fund reserves held two point

255
00:08:30,690 --> 00:08:32,384
five six trillion dollars,

256
00:08:32,384 --> 00:08:34,323
according to the trustees,

257
00:08:34,323 --> 00:08:37,269
as reported by the Congressional Research

258
00:08:37,269 --> 00:08:38,360
Service.

259
00:08:38,570 --> 00:08:40,713
But in twenty twenty-five,

260
00:08:40,713 --> 00:08:42,042
costs beat revenue,

261
00:08:42,042 --> 00:08:44,755
and the reserves shrank by one hundred

262
00:08:44,755 --> 00:08:47,253
and sixty billion dollars.

263
00:08:47,517 --> 00:08:49,917
Let me draw the round trip.

264
00:08:50,037 --> 00:08:53,007
Payroll taxes flow into the trust fund.

265
00:08:53,127 --> 00:08:56,123
Any surplus goes to the Treasury,

266
00:08:56,123 --> 00:08:58,097
which hands back an IOU.

267
00:08:58,307 --> 00:09:01,055
When benefits cost more than taxes bring

268
00:09:01,055 --> 00:09:01,577
in,

269
00:09:01,577 --> 00:09:03,473
the fund hands the IOU back,

270
00:09:03,473 --> 00:09:05,997
and the Treasury pays cash.

271
00:09:06,197 --> 00:09:07,470
To find that cash,

272
00:09:07,470 --> 00:09:09,890
it borrows from the public.

273
00:09:10,150 --> 00:09:12,640
Notice what happens to the total.

274
00:09:12,640 --> 00:09:13,504
Nothing,

275
00:09:13,504 --> 00:09:14,700
at first.

276
00:09:14,870 --> 00:09:17,338
The IOU just moves from the small

277
00:09:17,338 --> 00:09:19,480
box to the big box.

278
00:09:19,690 --> 00:09:22,552
Debt the government owed itself becomes

279
00:09:22,552 --> 00:09:24,822
debt it owes investors.

280
00:09:25,083 --> 00:09:27,693
And the drawer won't last forever.

281
00:09:27,693 --> 00:09:30,137
The trustees project the retirement fund's

282
00:09:30,137 --> 00:09:32,136
reserves run out in late twenty

283
00:09:32,136 --> 00:09:33,403
thirty-two.

284
00:09:33,583 --> 00:09:35,734
Counting the disability fund too,

285
00:09:35,734 --> 00:09:38,101
the combined reserves last into twenty

286
00:09:38,101 --> 00:09:39,293
thirty-four.

287
00:09:39,363 --> 00:09:40,213
After that,

288
00:09:40,213 --> 00:09:42,394
incoming taxes would cover about

289
00:09:42,394 --> 00:09:44,357
eighty-three percent of scheduled

290
00:09:44,357 --> 00:09:45,207
benefits,

291
00:09:45,207 --> 00:09:47,552
unless Congress changes the law.

292
00:09:51,817 --> 00:09:54,377
Which brings us to the meter.

293
00:09:54,427 --> 00:09:56,568
In fiscal year twenty twenty-six,

294
00:09:56,568 --> 00:09:58,276
which ended in September,

295
00:09:58,276 --> 00:10:00,417
the government's net interest bill,

296
00:10:00,417 --> 00:10:02,579
what it pays in interest minus what

297
00:10:02,579 --> 00:10:03,423
it collects,

298
00:10:03,423 --> 00:10:05,369
reached one point zero five trillion

299
00:10:05,369 --> 00:10:05,996
dollars,

300
00:10:05,996 --> 00:10:08,158
according to the Monthly Treasury

301
00:10:08,158 --> 00:10:09,407
Statement.

302
00:10:09,807 --> 00:10:11,485
That's about two point nine billion

303
00:10:11,485 --> 00:10:12,933
dollars every day,

304
00:10:12,933 --> 00:10:14,792
by our calculation.

305
00:10:15,050 --> 00:10:17,381
Now put national defense on the other

306
00:10:17,381 --> 00:10:18,960
side of the scale.

307
00:10:18,960 --> 00:10:19,691
Same year,

308
00:10:19,691 --> 00:10:21,190
same statement.

309
00:10:21,190 --> 00:10:23,072
Nine hundred and sixty-five billion

310
00:10:23,072 --> 00:10:24,020
dollars.

311
00:10:24,020 --> 00:10:25,670
Interest outweighed it.

312
00:10:25,670 --> 00:10:27,270
And it wasn't a one-off.

313
00:10:27,270 --> 00:10:29,167
In fiscal twenty twenty-five,

314
00:10:29,167 --> 00:10:31,513
interest was nine hundred and seventy

315
00:10:31,513 --> 00:10:32,557
billion dollars,

316
00:10:32,557 --> 00:10:33,389
and defense,

317
00:10:33,389 --> 00:10:35,307
nine hundred and seventeen billion

318
00:10:35,307 --> 00:10:36,334
dollars.

319
00:10:36,583 --> 00:10:38,753
Why did the meter speed up?

320
00:10:38,753 --> 00:10:39,873
Rates.

321
00:10:39,983 --> 00:10:41,852
In October twenty twenty-one,

322
00:10:41,852 --> 00:10:43,953
the average interest rate on the

323
00:10:43,953 --> 00:10:46,263
government's marketable debt was one point

324
00:10:46,263 --> 00:10:47,553
four six percent.

325
00:10:47,553 --> 00:10:49,847
By October twenty twenty-six,

326
00:10:49,847 --> 00:10:52,533
it was three point five seven percent.

327
00:10:52,683 --> 00:10:54,642
Cheap old bonds keep maturing,

328
00:10:54,642 --> 00:10:57,122
and they get replaced with pricier new

329
00:10:57,122 --> 00:10:58,218
ones.

330
00:10:58,483 --> 00:11:01,033
Let me put them side by side.

331
00:11:01,033 --> 00:11:01,651
Defense,

332
00:11:01,651 --> 00:11:03,533
fiscal twenty twenty-six.

333
00:11:03,533 --> 00:11:04,491
Net interest,

334
00:11:04,491 --> 00:11:05,853
a bit taller.

335
00:11:05,853 --> 00:11:06,563
Medicare,

336
00:11:06,563 --> 00:11:08,693
about the same height as interest.

337
00:11:08,693 --> 00:11:10,122
Social Security,

338
00:11:10,122 --> 00:11:11,343
taller still.

339
00:11:11,433 --> 00:11:13,592
Interest is now about fourteen cents of

340
00:11:13,592 --> 00:11:15,534
every federal dollar spent,

341
00:11:15,534 --> 00:11:17,312
by our calculation.

342
00:11:17,550 --> 00:11:19,730
And remember who sits on the other

343
00:11:19,730 --> 00:11:21,420
end of that meter.

344
00:11:21,540 --> 00:11:23,610
The holders we met tonight.

345
00:11:23,720 --> 00:11:24,745
Money funds,

346
00:11:24,745 --> 00:11:25,507
pensions,

347
00:11:25,507 --> 00:11:26,006
banks,

348
00:11:26,006 --> 00:11:27,031
households,

349
00:11:27,031 --> 00:11:27,793
the Fed,

350
00:11:27,793 --> 00:11:29,970
and investors abroad.

351
00:11:30,110 --> 00:11:31,878
Every tick of the meter is a

352
00:11:31,878 --> 00:11:33,716
payment to somebody.

353
00:11:33,950 --> 00:11:36,125
And the Congressional Budget Office

354
00:11:36,125 --> 00:11:38,900
expects the meter to keep speeding up.

355
00:11:39,090 --> 00:11:41,104
In its February baseline,

356
00:11:41,104 --> 00:11:43,141
net interest climbs from three point three

357
00:11:43,141 --> 00:11:45,631
percent of the economy this year to

358
00:11:45,631 --> 00:11:47,441
four point six percent by twenty

359
00:11:47,441 --> 00:11:48,720
thirty-six.

360
00:11:49,010 --> 00:11:50,089
The previous high,

361
00:11:50,089 --> 00:11:52,049
going back to nineteen forty,

362
00:11:52,049 --> 00:11:53,568
was three point two percent,

363
00:11:53,568 --> 00:11:55,710
in nineteen ninety-one.

364
00:11:55,850 --> 00:11:57,175
That's a projection,

365
00:11:57,175 --> 00:11:58,673
not a result.

366
00:11:58,917 --> 00:12:02,177
So who actually owns America's debt?

367
00:12:02,177 --> 00:12:02,862
Not China,

368
00:12:02,862 --> 00:12:04,397
at least not much of it.

369
00:12:04,397 --> 00:12:06,807
Foreign investors hold about a quarter,

370
00:12:06,807 --> 00:12:09,027
and Japan holds more than China.

371
00:12:09,197 --> 00:12:11,337
The Fed holds about a ninth.

372
00:12:11,337 --> 00:12:13,582
The government owes almost a fifth to

373
00:12:13,582 --> 00:12:14,174
itself,

374
00:12:14,174 --> 00:12:16,407
mostly to future retirees.

375
00:12:16,457 --> 00:12:18,345
And most of the rest belongs to

376
00:12:18,345 --> 00:12:19,501
Americans,

377
00:12:19,501 --> 00:12:20,657
through money funds,

378
00:12:20,657 --> 00:12:21,105
banks,

379
00:12:21,105 --> 00:12:23,357
pensions and their own savings.

380
00:12:23,467 --> 00:12:25,633
The biggest lender to the United States

381
00:12:25,633 --> 00:12:27,487
is the United States.

382
00:12:27,487 --> 00:12:31,399
So every dollar of interest is two

383
00:12:31,399 --> 00:12:32,767
things at once.

384
00:12:32,767 --> 00:12:33,870
A cost for taxpayers,

385
00:12:33,870 --> 00:12:36,151
and income for whoever holds the IOU.

386
00:12:36,417 --> 00:12:36,989
Next time,

387
00:12:36,989 --> 00:12:39,550
we follow the money behind another hidden

388
00:12:39,550 --> 00:12:41,727
link of the world economy.

389
00:12:41,837 --> 00:12:44,051
This is The Hidden Economics.
